# bc_admits.ledger — PROGRAMS rustc REJECTS AND logosc COMPILES, named one per
# row. THIS FILE IS THE ACTIONABLE QUEUE: every row here is a borrow-check
# DEFECT with a root, and a round may pick its targets from it without first
# re-deciding whether the row is a defect at all.
#
# ⚠ IT IS NO LONGER THE ONLY PLACE THE ADMITTED SET IS NAMED — 2026-09-04. The
# rows that NO COMPILER FIX CLOSES (the port is legal Rust as written; a
# canonised Logos divergence; a wall of legal fixtures only the owner can move)
# were split out into `tests/logos/bc_admits_blocked.ledger`, 124 -> 99 + 25.
# The ADMITTED SET is the two files together, and the gate reads both: every
# row has a file, every file under tests/imported/admit/ has a row in ONE of
# them, and each file holds its OWN `# TOTAL`. Nothing left the admit shelf, so
# every one of the 124 programs still has its `logos_00_bc_admit_*` test and a
# program that stops being admitted is still RED wherever its row lives.
#
# ⚠ THE BLOCKED FILE IS RE-VERIFIED, NOT CARRIED. A DECLINE IS A MEASUREMENT
# WITH A TIMESTAMP AND A WALL DECAYS — this split exists because a "five owner
# fixtures wall this block" verdict was carried for six rounds after four of the
# five had been REPAIRED, and re-pricing came back 5 -> 1. Every blocked row
# carries the DATE its reason was last checked against the tree; a reason that
# cannot be re-checked today sends its row BACK here. Moving a row THERE needs
# the port and the wall opened today, not a citation.
#
# Source: 983 rustc compile-fail tests from tests/ui/{borrowck,nll,moves,
# lifetimes,regions,dropck,drop}, ported at upstream
# da5114692c9ebe46b869488c5f34f92eb10b98c1 (src/version 1.100.0), batch B167.
# The ports that logosc refuses FOR THE UPSTREAM REASON are ordinary fail
# fixtures under tests/imported/fail/ and are not listed here. These are the
# rest: each is a borrow-check hole, and the `root` column is the root cause the
# import blocks isolated with a control that refuses.
#
# Format:  <fixture> <root-id> <path relative to the repo root, no .logos>
#
# `root-id` is BLOCK-QUALIFIED (`bck.` / `nllmoves.` / `lifereg.`) because the
# class letters are per-block names, not global identities — the same letter
# means different things in two blocks, and a bare `B` would silently merge
# them. Roots, per the block reports:
#   A  a PARAMETER is not in the tracked set          D  a TEMPORARY has no owner to key a loan on
#   B  bookkeeping is ROOT-keyed and does not follow  E  a DROP-ORDER interaction is not modelled
#      a projection (field / index / deref hop)       L* lifetime-specific roots (lifereg block)
#   C  the CLOSURE BOUNDARY deposits nothing          N*/R*/NEW* roots proposed by their slice
#
# Held in BOTH directions by `logos_00_bc_admits_ledger`
# (tests/logos/bc_admits_ledger_gate.sh): a listed program that STOPS being
# admitted is RED — a fixed defect must DELETE its row and land the program as
# a fail fixture. The `# TOTAL` line below is held against the row count, so
# neither adding nor removing a row can be silent.
#
# ⚠ A ROW IS NOT AN EXCUSE. It is a defect with a name, and the intended way for
# this file to change is to SHRINK: a class fix reds this gate BECAUSE IT
# IMPROVED, and the count is then edited down in the same change.
# 2026-09-08b — 91 -> 91 (ONE ROW CLOSED, ONE ROW OPENED, and they are different
# programs). `bck.A-REFPARAM` is CLOSED: `mut-borrow-of-mut-ref` is deleted and
# its program is now a `fail` fixture at tests/imported/fail/borrowck/.
#   THE CLASS, ENUMERATED BY THE PROPERTY. The hatch in `refuse_not_mut_binding`
#   exempts a PARAMETER from the binding-mut check, and its own comment says why:
#   "a `&mut` through a reference param is a REBORROW". A borrow that names the
#   BINDING ITSELF is not a reborrow. So the class is not "rows about &mut on a
#   param" — it is every arrival at that hatch with the borrow taken OF the
#   binding, and the fix is to ask that property instead of asking the identity
#   `param_names_`. The by-VALUE half of the same hatch was closed 2026-08-31p by
#   the same argument; this is its reference half, and the hatch now has no
#   unconditional exit left.
#   ⚠ THE PROPERTY IS A FACT ABOUT THE LOWERING, NOT ABOUT BorrowPlace, AND THAT
#   WAS MEASURED THE WRONG WAY FIRST. `bp.path.empty() && !bp.through_ref` LOOKS
#   like "the place is the binding"; it is also true for `&mut *b` and for the
#   implicit reborrow at a call arg, because the Deref arm roots such a place at
#   the reference VARIABLE on purpose. An arm keyed on it refused the stdlib and
#   all eight legal hand programs. `Code::AddrOf` — `&mut b` on a bare variable —
#   is the one lowering that borrows the binding; the reborrow lowering is
#   `AddrOfTemp(Deref(VarRef))`. RecordFlags::of_binding carries it.
#   MEASURED (ceiling probe `mbrefself`, build 931): fires 6, CEILING 1 row,
#   COST 0 legal programs over pass(ledger+legal) + fail(text) + stdlib(4 layers).
#   Predicted BY NAME before the build: {mut-borrow-of-mut-ref}. predicted∖closed
#   = ∅, closed∖predicted = ∅.
#   HAND ORACLE, 18 programs in 14 shapes (rule 5 — not all of one syntax): 14
#   legal still compile (implicit reborrow at a call, `*b = 5`, field write, a
#   `&mut self` method on a ref param, explicit `&mut *b`, a `&mut self` ->
#   `&mut self` chain, index write through `&mut [i64;4]`, `&mut b.f`, a generic
#   `&mut T` param, returning `&mut b.f`, a closure capturing a ref param, a
#   struct literal holding it, `&mut *b` twice, a `&mut [i64]` slice param); 4
#   illegal are refused (`&mut b` on `&mut i64`, on `&i64`, on `&S`, and into a
#   `&mut &mut i64` let).
#
