# 2026-09-08 — 95 -> 94. `bck.NEW-SUBSLICE` CLOSES, AND THE ROOT THE ROW CARRIED
# FOR EIGHT DAYS NAMED THE WRONG SITE. The 2026-08-30 note above says this row is
# "a whole-value use of a partially-moved array at a match SCRUTINEE ... at a site
# `take_borrow_whole_` already touches". MEASURED TODAY, it is neither: the
# whole-value reader is LIVE and correct — `match a { [_, _, x] => … } a[0] = …`
# (a DISJOINT index, base build b74842a0d915249f) is already refused with "use of
# partially moved value 'a' (field '2' moved on line 6)", which is what
# `hp_disjoint` was recorded as ADMITTING on 2026-08-30. What deposited nothing
# was the PRODUCER: `each_pat_binding_place`'s PC::Slice arm handed the `xs @ ..`
# rest the CONTAINER's place and a NULL type, so `is_move_type` was false and the
# by-value sub-place move rule skipped it. Prefix and suffix elements had carried
# an index place and the element type since 2026-08-30; the rest had not.
# THE CLASS, ENUMERATED BY THE PROPERTY ("a by-value slice-pattern binder that
# consumes elements of an OWNED array"), not by spelling. Three binder forms x
# three doors, each compiled on the base binary with the same probe (move, then
# `a[0u64] = …`):
#   prefix element `[x, _, _]`      match ✓ refused   if-let ✓ refused
#   suffix element `[_, .., x]`     match ✓ refused   if-let ✓ refused
#   rest binder    `[_, _, x @ ..]` match ✗ ADMITTED  if-let ✗ ADMITTED
#   rest binder    `[x @ ..]`       match ✗ ADMITTED  (full cover)
#   rest binder    `[_, x @ .., _]` match ✗ ADMITTED  (middle cover)
# The `let` door is not a member in either direction: `let [x, _, _] = a;`
# consumes the WHOLE array ("use of moved variable 'a'"), which is coarser than
# rustc, not permissive. `ref x @ ..` is not a member either — it is a SYNTAX
# ERROR on this tree (rc 4, "syntax error near '..'"), so the rest slot cannot
# hold a RefBind and the arm has no by-reference case to decline. So the class is
# THREE cover shapes at TWO doors, one missing fact, and one structural change
# closes all five: the rest names the indices [prefix, n - suffix) with the
# element type. All five refuse on the armed build, each with its own diagnostic
# read (hp_t13, hp_full_rest, hp_mid_rest, hp_rest_twice, hp_iflet_rest).
# ⚠ THE PLACE MUST STAY PER-ELEMENT. Giving the rest the CONTAINER's place with
# a type makes it a whole-value consume and refuses `match a { [_, _, x @ ..] =>
# … } match a { [y, _, _] => … }` — disjoint move paths, legal in Rust, admitted
# at HEAD. Pinned as pass/bc_subslice_disjoint_elem_legal against
# fail/bc_subslice_rest_elem_moved_twice, whose middle rest DOES cover the index
# the second pattern moves and which names `a.1`, not `a.0` and not the array.
# PREDICTED BY NAME BEFORE THE ARMED BINARY EXISTED, closed exactly:
# predicted∖closed = ∅, closed∖predicted = ∅ (one row).
# ⚠ AND A DECLINE, WITH THE READING THAT CONDEMNS IT — `bck.D` + `nllmoves.D`,
# the 7-row block this round was pointed at first. READ, NOT MEASURED: the seven
# ports carry their own upstream annotation, and by ERROR CODE they are two
# populations, not one.
#   E0502/E0499, a loan conflict INSIDE one expression (3):
#     two-phase-nonrecv-autoref--a-fnmut-twice   `f(f(10))` through `&mut F`
