# ── 2026-09-09 — THE E0502/E0499 HALF OF `bck.D` PRICED, 90 -> 90, NO ROW CLOSED,
#    AND THE MERGE'S OWN GLOSS REFUTED BY ONE HAND PROGRAM (PROBES.md 2026-09-09m-tpb)
# Priced as the DECLINE above ordered: 3, not 7. `lifereg.R2`'s two rows are priced
# WITH them because that block's own 2026-09-08 re-port note claims the same mechanism.
# NO COMPILER SOURCE WAS TOUCHED — both arms were already `probe::on` gates at HEAD.
#
# ⚠ "A TEMPORARY HAS NO OWNER TO KEY A LOAN ON" IS FALSE FOR THIS HALF. Measured on
# build 58fd3c9773b9c475: `two_mut(&mut a, &mut a)` — BOTH loans pure argument
# temporaries — is REFUSED, "cannot borrow 'a' as mutable: already mutably borrowed".
# The discriminator over eight shapes is the MUTABILITY MIX, not temporariness:
# `&mut`+`&mut` inline refuses; `&mut`+`&` inline admits in EITHER order (scalar,
# array, struct, three-arg, method receiver); the same mixed pair sequenced into lets
# refuses. The cause is the DELIBERATE two-phase reservation at
# borrow_check.cpp::take_borrow, whose own comment states the exemption.
#
# THE ARRIVAL CENSUS, ALL 98 ADMIT PROGRAMS, TWO EXISTING census BUCKETS, NO EDIT:
#   argresvsib/shared_over_reservation  --c-mut-and-shared-args, --c26, --t26   (3 of 98)
#   argresvact/shared_live_at_call      --c-mut-and-shared-args                 (1 of 98)
# `--a-fnmut-twice` and `--d-index-two-phase` are ZERO AT BOTH DOORS — the first kind
# of zero, an UNREACHED SITE, proven live by the other 20+ buckets the same compile
# fired. SO THE BLOCK IS FOUR MECHANISMS, and two of its five rows are MIS-ROOTED:
#   --d  a user `Index::index` call mints NO shared borrow of its receiver root.
#        ⚠ Its sequencing control (`let k = i[3]; i[k] = 4;`) is LEGAL Rust and admits,
#        so --d has no separating pair by sequencing, unlike every other row here.
#   --a  a call through a `FnMut` VALUE mints no borrow of the callee. Cheapest carrier
#        is not the ledger program: `let mut c = |x: i64| { return x + 1i64; };
#        let r = c(c(10i64));` — E0499 upstream, silent here, ZERO borrow records.
#        ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-09-12f — THAT CARRIER IS **LEGAL RUST**, NOT E0499, AND A
#        ROUND THAT TOOK IT AT ITS WORD WOULD HAVE BOUGHT THIS ROW WITH A
#        LEGAL-PROGRAM REFUSAL. A closure with NO CAPTURES resolves to `call`, not
#        `call_mut`, and upstream's own comment at tests/ui/borrowck/
#        two-phase-nonrecv-autoref.rs:39-49 says so in as many words: "or we will
#        resolve both to `call` (which will just work, regardless of two-phase
#        borrow support)". The same file's `twice_ten_si<F: Fn>(f: &mut F) { f(f(10)) }`
#        carries NO `//~ ERROR` (line 54), and the identical program is already a
#        GREEN pass fixture in this tree: tests/imported/pass/closures/
#        closure-fn-bound-twice-cl2. It was measured as one of the TWO costs of
#        ceiling probe `calleeresv` (PROBES.md 2026-09-12f). ⚠ No rustc BINARY is on
#        this box; the verdict rests on upstream's `//~ ERROR` annotations and that
#        comment, both read at da5114692c9. THE ROW IS UNTOUCHED — only this note.
#        THE REAL CARRIER IS THE LEDGER PROGRAM, and what the arm needs is the
#        Fn-FAMILY KIND, which `borrow_check.cpp` does not carry at all (zero
#        occurrences of FnMut / FnOnce / closure_kind / is_fn_family).
#
# THE TWO ARMS, BOTH ALREADY IN THE TREE AND UNARMED (ceiling-probe.sh, full cost line):
#   argresvsibshared  fires 26  CEILING 3 (--c, --c26, --t26; predicted BY NAME,
#                     predicted∖closed = ∅, closed∖predicted = ∅)  COST 8 legal +
#                     1 text-only fail fixture + ⛔ `logos.mem` DOES NOT COMPILE
#                     (10 refusals, first at `alt_expr_0`). CONDEMNED: its eight costs
#                     ARE the two-phase corpus, and `v.push(v.len())` arrives at that
#                     door with count 1. Rust's two-phase borrow exists to admit it.
#   argresvact        fires 2   CEILING 1 (--c; predicted BY NAME, ∅ / ∅)
#                     COST-fail 0 of 1478, STDLIB all four layers build,
#                     COST(pass) = ONE fixture — see the corpus decision below.
#                     Nine hand programs, shapes varied: it refuses `ms(&mut a, &a)`,
#                     `sm(&a, &mut a)`, the struct root, the three-argument form and
#                     the ledger row, and correctly ADMITS `v.push(v.len())`,
#                     `s.set(s.get())` (upstream's own "But this is okay") and
#                     `wr(&mut s, rd(&s))`.
#
# ⚠ A PASS FIXTURE ASSERTS THE EXACT CONSTRUCT OF A LEDGER ROW — CORPUS DECISION,
#   OWNER'S, REPORTED AND NOT EDITED. `argresvact`'s entire cost is
#   tests/imported/pass/nll/tpb-mut-with-shared-ref-arg.logos:
#       fn write_then_read(m: &mut i32, r: &i32) -> i32 { *m = *r; return *m; }
#       write_then_read(&mut x, &x)
#   which is `double_access(&mut a, &a)` with a scalar root — the construct of row
#   `two-phase-nonrecv-autoref--c-mut-and-shared-args`, pinned GREEN one shelf over.
#   READ AT THE SOURCE, not cited: rustc @ da5114692c9,
#   tests/ui/borrowck/two-phase-nonrecv-autoref.rs lines 104-112 mark it
#   `//~ ERROR ... [E0502]` and mark the NEXT line `a.m(a.i(10))` "But this is okay".
#   While that fixture stands, `ceiling-probe.sh` reads COST >= CEILING and stops the
#   arm — correctly on the number, wrongly on the meaning.
#
#   ⚠ AND A SECOND ONE, VISIBLE ONLY TO THE RUNTIME COLUMN. run_oracle.py over 6602
#   pass fixtures compiled+linked+RUN, base and armed from ONE configure: THREE rows
#   differ — `cast-region-to-uint` (stack address, subtracted by name),
#   `tpb-mut-with-shared-ref-arg`, and `tests/imported/pass/cmp/test_harness_coretest_cmp`,
#   which is labelled outside `-L bc -L pass` and so is invisible to ceiling-probe.sh.
#   Its line 125 is `let mut a: i32 = 5i32; assert_eq!((&mut a).cmp(&a), Ordering::Equal);`
#   — `&mut a` and `&a` in one expression, E0502 upstream. Upstream's test_mut_int_totalord
#   is `(&mut 5).cmp(&&mut 5)` over TEMPORARIES; the port replaced them with one named
#   local and made the program illegal. SECOND corpus decision, reported, not edited.
#   So argresvact's honest cost line is pass 1 / fail-text 0 / stdlib 0 / RUNTIME 2, and
#   both runtime rows are corpus decisions rather than damage.
#
# WHY --c26/--t26 READ ZERO AT `argresvact` — the SECOND kind of zero, not the first.
# `record_borrow` deposits the bare-place receiver's reservation inside its own
# `in_call_args_++ / --` bracket, which runs BEFORE `visit_args` pushes the call-args
# frame; `argresvact` iterates that frame AFTER `each_arg`, so the receiver's mut
# reservation is in the wrong frame and the gate never asks. One line of scope
# bookkeeping, not a new predicate.
#
# FUNDING: `argresvact` + the receiver reservation visible at activation. Predicted
# ceiling 3, and it also closes `a.bump(&a)` — a hole in NO corpus. ⚠ RULE 13: the
# halves are in SERIES (the receiver half alone has no arm and prices 0), so price the
# pair WHOLE. ⚠ The widened form must be re-priced against `logos.mem`. ⚠ RULE "a row
# closed by a wrong diagnostic is not closed": `argresvact` says "cannot borrow 'a' as
# mutable: N shared borrow(s) active"; upstream's E0502 blames the SHARED side.
# DO NOT FUND `argresvsibshared` in any crude form.
#
# ── 2026-09-09 — DECLINE, AND THE CLASS IS ONE RULE WITH FOUR MEMBERS, THREE ──
#    OF WHICH NEVER REACH THE ARM. NO COMPILER SOURCE TOUCHED THIS ROUND.
#
# The previous round recommended funding `argresvact` + the receiver-frame fix as a
# pair. The recommendation is DECLINED here, on a number that was already in this
# file and is re-verified below, not on a new measurement of the mechanism.
#
# THE CLASS, ENUMERATED BY PROPERTY — "a `&mut` reservation on root R activates at
# a call while a shared borrow of R is still live" (rustc E0502). NOT by spelling:
# every member below was found by asking where a mut reservation can come from, and
# each was compiled on HEAD 7f8ac5902 with LOGOS_CENSUS reading the arm's buckets.
#   1. explicit `&mut` argument + shared argument   two-phase-nonrecv-autoref--c
#      ARRIVES: argresvact/shared_live_at_call 1. Closed by the arm.
#   2. BARE-place receiver autoref                  NO ROW HERE — filed 2026-09-09 as
#      soundness_queue row `method_bare_recv_mut_resv_shared_arg_admits`.
#      0 arrivals (128 = the empty-program prelude baseline). UNREACHED SITE.
#   3. receiver through a DEREF place op            regions-adjusted-lvalue-op--c26
#      0 arrivals. UNREACHED SITE.
#   4. receiver through an INDEX place op           regions-adjusted-lvalue-op--t26
#      0 arrivals. UNREACHED SITE.
# Members 2-4 are ONE bookkeeping defect, already diagnosed in the note above: the
# receiver's reservation is deposited in the scope OUTSIDE the call-args frame the
# arm iterates. The rule that decides all four is the same rule, so implementing it
# for the receiver only and not for the explicit argument would be a fix BY SPELLING
# — refused under the owner's 2026-09-05 "fix the class, not the instance".
#
# ⚠ MEMBER 2 IS A DOOR THAT LOST ITS CARRIER TO AN AS-IS RE-PORT. Until the
# 2026-09-08 stage-6 re-port, `regions-adjusted-lvalue-op--c26` was written
# `v.oh_no(&v)` — the bare-place spelling. The re-port to upstream's DEREF door was
# CORRECT (upstream's tests/ui/regions/regions-adjusted-lvalue-op.rs, READ on the box
# at da5114692c9, contains only `v[0].oh_no(&v)` and `(*v).oh_no(&v)`), and nothing
# was filed for the door the old port had been occupying. Re-measured 2026-09-09:
# both `v.oh_no(&v)` (Vec receiver) and `s.bump(&s)` (struct receiver) compile rc 0.
#
# THE NUMBER THAT CONDEMNS THE LANDING, RE-VERIFIED ON HEAD 7f8ac5902 BY HAND:
#   tests/imported/pass/nll/tpb-mut-with-shared-ref-arg.logos      base rc 0, armed rc 1
#     "cannot borrow 'x' as mutable: 1 shared borrow(s) active"
#   tests/imported/pass/cmp/test_harness_coretest_cmp.logos        base rc 0, armed rc 1
#     "cannot borrow 'a' as mutable: 1 shared borrow(s) active" in test_mut_int_totalord
# TWO green pass fixtures, both asserting constructs rustc refuses with E0502 (the
# first read at the source: two-phase-nonrecv-autoref.rs lines 105-108 mark
# `double_access(&mut a, &a)` `//~ ERROR ... [E0502]` and the NEXT line `a.m(a.i(10))`
# "But this is okay"; the second is `(&mut a).cmp(&a)` on one named local at line 125,
# where upstream's test_mut_int_totalord uses TEMPORARIES). Both are corpus decisions
# with an owner and may not be edited to buy a row. While they stand, the class is
# blocked — not for want of a mechanism.
#
# ⚠ RULE 5 IS DISCHARGED FOR `argresvact` ON THE LEGAL SIDE, AND THE PRICING ROUND'S
# NINE HAND PROGRAMS WERE NOT ENOUGH TO SAY SO. Fifteen fresh legal programs, in
# shapes neither prompt named — disjoint struct fields `f(&mut s.a, &s.b)`; a shared
# borrow dead before the call; a shared borrow scoped to a closed inner block; two
# roots; `wr(&mut a, rd(&a))`; a Copy argument; a reborrow passed through a `&mut`
# parameter; the same call in a `while` loop; `&self` receiver with a shared argument;
# `&mut` receiver with a shared argument of ANOTHER root; the shared borrow consumed by
# the FIRST argument with `&mut` second; a live shared borrow of another root across the
# call; `s.add(s.a)`; two sequential `&mut` calls; `&mut *b` through a Box deref.
# ALL FIFTEEN: base rc 0, armed rc 0. NO false refusal.
# ⚠ FOUR OF THE FIFTEEN ARE UNREACHED-SITE ZEROS AND PROVE NOTHING ABOUT THE ARM —
# the disjoint-fields, `&self`-receiver, other-root-receiver and `s.add(s.a)` programs
# all read 128 = baseline (field borrows live in `field_borrows`, which the arm does
# not iterate; receivers are in the wrong frame). The other ELEVEN reach
# argresvact/reserved at +1 or +2 over baseline and are correctly admitted.
# On the illegal side five fresh programs: `g(&mut a, &a)` and `h(&a, &mut a, &a)` are
# NEWLY refused by the arm (2 and 1 arrivals at shared_live_at_call); `f(&mut s.a, &s)`
# and `(&mut s).cmpish(&s)` were ALREADY refused unarmed, by other rules, so the arm
# INHERITS them and buys nothing there; `s.bump(&s)` is member 2 and is missed.
#
# STILL DECLINED, AND FOR A DIFFERENT REASON — NOT THIS CLASS AT ALL:
#   two-phase-nonrecv-autoref--a-fnmut-twice     0 arrivals at either door
#   two-phase-nonrecv-autoref--d-index-two-phase 0 arrivals at either door
# Both need a PRODUCER first (a call through a `FnMut` value; a user `Index::index`
# receiver), as the 2026-09-09m note already said. Re-verified 2026-09-09: both still
# admit, armed and unarmed.
#
# WHAT THE BLOCK NEEDS, IN ORDER: (1) the owner's ruling on the two pass fixtures —
# both are mis-ports of programs upstream refuses, and NEITHER is a deliberate
# divergence anyone recorded a reason for; (2) then the receiver reservation moved into
# the call-args frame, which is the one change that turns members 2-4 from unreached
# sites into arrivals; (3) then the diagnostic rewritten to blame the SHARED side, as
# E0502 does, since a row closed by a wrong sentence is not closed. Steps 2 and 3 are
# cheap and already understood. Step 1 is not mine.
#
# ── 2026-09-11 — `lifereg.NEW-E0207` PRICED FOR THE FIRST TIME: 3 / 0 / 0 of ──
#    1478 / 4 of 4 / 0 of 6646, AND THE ONE THING THAT BLOCKS IT IS THE SENTENCE
#
# NO ROW MOVED AND NO COMPILER SOURCE IS CHANGED — the probe was installed,
# measured and REVERTED, and the revert is asserted by behaviour as well as by a
# re-read build hash. `# TOTAL` stays 90.
#
# Three rows, ONE construct: `-5` and `-6` are byte-identical in the program body
# and `-1` differs in ONE token (`&S` vs `&'_ S`). The grouping was TESTED, not
# assumed — one candidate change moves all three, and it did.
#
# THE NARROW ARM IS RUSTC'S OWN RULE: an impl lifetime binder constrained by
# neither the self type nor the trait args, AND named by an ASSOCIATED TYPE.
# ceiling 3 (exactly these three rows, predicted by name, both differences empty),
# cost 0 on pass+ledger, 0 of 1478 fail-text, all four stdlib layers, and 0 of 6646
# at RUN time (the single differing row is `cast-region-to-uint`, the named
# stack-address exclusion).
#
# THE CRUDE ARM IS DECLINED AND ITS EXTRA ROW IS NOT A ROW. `e0207unc` (any
# unconstrained binder) closes a fourth, `trait-associated-constant` — but
# upstream's .stderr for it, READ on the box, is **E0308 "const not compatible
# with trait"**, not E0207: rustc builds its `lifetimes_in_associated_types` set
# from associated TYPES, not consts. Honest ceiling 3 at cost 1
# (`tests/logos/pass/bc_mcallvar_legal_twins`, a binder used only in a METHOD
# SIGNATURE — legal Rust). Strictly worse than the narrow arm on both columns.
#
# ⚠ THE PREDICATE IS TWO DOORS IN SERIES AND THE SECOND HAS NO CARRIER.
# `sema_decl.cpp` ~2971 DISCARDS a lifetime at a trait-argument position by
# design — "L1: skip LIFETIME_PARAM at trait-arg position; Logos doesn't track
