# ── 2026-09-12f — 85 -> 84. `issue-27868` CLOSED. AN ARGUMENT-POSITION `&mut` ─
#    DEPOSITS A *RESERVATION*, AND `Code::Assign`'s TWO EXCLUSIVITY ARMS READ
#    ONLY THE OTHER RECORD
#
# THE FIX, seven lines at ONE site (`borrow_check.cpp::visit_stmt`, Code::Assign):
#   else if (!said_shared && it->mut_reservations > 0)
#       report(ln, "cannot assign to '{}' because it is borrowed", name);
# — rustc's own E0506 sentence, READ on the armed binary against upstream's
# `tests/ui/nll/issue-27868.stderr`. The port moves admit/ -> fail/nll/ with that
# sentence as its `.expected`.
#
# THE CLASS, BY THE PROPERTY, NOT BY THE SPELLING. `tools/dlog/selftest.sh` rc 0,
# known answer reproduced (19 walkers / 24 findings / try_path 1-5 / domain 42-5).
# New rule `tools/dlog/resv_write_arms.dl` over stmt::Code, TWO independent
# mechanical domains because one is a property chosen by hand:
#   D1 "the arm calls place_write_loans"  -> 7 arms, and it MISSES Code::Assign,
#      which is this round's own target — recorded, because it is exactly the
#      premature-closure failure the tool exists for.
#   D2 "the arm reads mut_borrowed / shared_borrows" -> Assign, IndexWrite,
#      FieldIndexWrite, DerefWrite.
#   UNION = 8 candidate arms; 5 read the reservation fact NOWHERE:
#      ChainFieldWrite · DerefFieldWrite · DerefWrite · FieldWrite · TupleWrite.
# PER-SITE READ, which is the cross-check the `ctx_of` coarsening requires — one
# hand program per candidate spelling, all on the BASE binary:
#      f(&mut arr, { arr[0] = 9; 0 })      REFUSED   (IndexWrite spelling)
#      f(&mut h,   { h.xs[0] = 9; 0 })     REFUSED   (FieldIndexWrite)
#      f(&mut h,   { h.n = 9; 0 })         REFUSED   (DerefWrite)
#      f(&mut o,   { o.i.n = 9; 0 })       REFUSED   (ChainFieldWrite spelling)
#      f(&mut t,   { t.0 = 9; 0 })         REFUSED   (TupleWrite spelling)
#      f(&mut x,   { x = 5; 0 })           ADMITTED  <- scalar
#      f(&mut h,   { h = Holder{9}; 0 })   ADMITTED  <- struct
#      f(&mut arr, { arr = [8,9]; 0 })     ADMITTED  <- array
# So the class is LIVE at exactly ONE arm with THREE carriers: every PROJECTION
# write takes an `&mut` of its own place and dies one door earlier, on
# take_borrow_whole_'s "already mutably borrowed" reservation conflict. The
# whole-value `Code::Assign` is not a borrow and reaches no such door. DECLINED BY
# NAME, with the number: `Code::IndexWrite` and `Code::FieldIndexWrite` — 0
# arrivals in 9650 tree programs (2026-09-12e census) and 5 of 5 hand spellings
# already refused; their probes `argresviw` / `argresvfiw` / `argresvwrite` are
# DELETED in this commit rather than left as arms no program executes.
#
# WHY THIS IS SEPARABLE FROM THE `argresvact` WALL (bck.D, owner-blocked on two
# green pass pins): that plane is about WHICH receivers may deposit a reservation
# and whether a READ or a SHARED BORROW of a reserved place is legal — and a
# two-phase reservation exists precisely to permit those. This arm refuses a
# WRITE, which Rust refuses under both readings of the deposit. The read
# (E0503) and shared-borrow (E0502) arms of the same table STAY OPEN and are not
# bought here.
#
# COST, all columns, one configure: pass 0 · fail corpus 0 (rc, `.expected`,
# normalised-stderr sha) · stdlib 4 of 4 layers · runtime 0 over the run_oracle
# population. RULE 5: 10 legal shapes, deliberately in 10 different syntaxes —
# two-phase receiver + shared read · a write to a DIFFERENT var in the same arg
# list · a write after the call · a write to a block-local · two reservations
# then a write · a reservation in a `while` body · a method receiver then a
# field AND a whole-value write · nested calls on two roots · branches with the
